---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Ambiguous = `We had seven applicants and I have their scores. Give me the
ones worth interviewing, best first.`;

const Questions = `Questions this brief does not answer

  - Worth interviewing above what score? Nobody said.
  - How many do I show? "Best first" implies a few, not all.
  - What if more people clear the bar than I am showing?
    Silently drop them, or say how many are hiding?
  - Two applicants tied on 88. Who goes first?

MY DECISIONS
  - Cutoff is a parameter, default 75. Not hardcoded.
  - Show 3, and print a count of how many more cleared the bar.
  - Ties keep their original order. Stated at the top of the file.`;

const pool = [
  { slug: "cc-roll-call", title: "Roll Call", stars: 1, after: "2.9" },
  { slug: "cc-high-score", title: "High Score", stars: 1, after: "2.9" },
  { slug: "cc-word-count", title: "Log Counter", stars: 2, after: "2.9" },
  { slug: "cc-seat-finder", title: "Seat Finder", stars: 2, after: "2.10" },
  { slug: "cc-podium", title: "Podium", stars: 2, after: "2.12" },
  { slug: "cc-step-counter", title: "Step Counter", stars: 2, after: "2.12" },
  { slug: "cc-merge-lists", title: "Merge", stars: 3, after: "2.12" },
  { slug: "cc-shortlist", title: "Shortlist", stars: 3, after: "2.12" },
];

const bonus = [
  { slug: "cc-bubble-sort", title: "Bubble Sort", stars: 3, after: "2.12" },
  { slug: "cc-number-guesser", title: "Number Guesser", stars: 3, after: "2.10" },
];
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Code Challenge Set 6</h1>

        <h4 class="text-sn-theme">Complete any 5 of the 8 below.</h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Set Is For</h2>

          <p>
            Set 5 was about the shape of a procedure. <b>This one is about
            picking the algorithm.</b>
          </p>

          <p>
            Not one of these briefs says <i>use binary search</i> or{" "}
            <i>sort it first</i>. They describe a job. Working out which
            algorithm the job needs &mdash; and whether the data lets you use it
            &mdash; is the whole exercise.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Two of them will punish you for choosing wrong in a way you can
            see.</b> Seat Finder gives a confident wrong answer if the list is
            not sorted. Merge gets an angry note from the client if you glue and
            sort.
          </p>

          <p>
            Same rule as always: <b>write your requirements list before you
            write any code</b>, and submit both.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="shape">
          <h2>The Brief Never Names the Algorithm</h2>

          <p>
            It also, often, does not name the numbers. Here is the whole
            Shortlist brief:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Ambiguous}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="From the Shortlist brief"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            Twenty-two words, and at least four unanswered questions inside them.
            Here is what should end up in your requirements list:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Questions}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Requirements — the questions half"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b>Notice the last decision.</b> Ties are the sort of thing that
            never comes up until real data arrives with one in it. A requirements
            list that spots the tie before the code does is doing its job.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>And notice the first one.</b> The cutoff became a parameter rather
            than a number typed into the middle of the procedure. That is the
            habit Set 5 drilled, still applying.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="counting">
          <h2>Most of These Ask You to Count</h2>

          <p>
            Log Counter, Seat Finder, Merge, and Step Counter all want a number
            of steps reported alongside the answer. That is not busywork.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Reporting the cost is how you tell whether you picked the right
            algorithm.</b> A search that always takes eight checks on eight items
            is not a binary search, whatever you called the procedure. The count
            is the evidence.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="challenges">
          <h2>The Challenges</h2>

          <p>Pick <b>any five</b>.</p>

          <ul class="list-unstyled mt-4">
            {
              pool.map((c) => (
                <li class="mb-2">
                  <i class="si-terminal text-sn-theme me-2"></i>
                  <a href={`/courses/ap-csp/unit-2/${c.slug}`}>{c.title}</a>
                  <span class="ms-2">
                    {Array.from({ length: c.stars }).map(() => (
                      <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
                    ))}
                  </span>
                  <span class="text-body-secondary ms-2">
                    &mdash; after Activity {c.after}
                  </span>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="bonus">
          <h2>If Five Is Not Enough</h2>

          <p>
            These two are <b>extra credit</b>, worth 3 points each on top of the
            set.
          </p>

          <ul class="list-unstyled mt-4">
            {
              bonus.map((c) => (
                <li class="mb-2">
                  <i class="si-flask text-sn-yellow me-2"></i>
                  <a href={`/courses/ap-csp/unit-2/${c.slug}`}>{c.title}</a>
                  <span class="ms-2">
                    {Array.from({ length: c.stars }).map(() => (
                      <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
                    ))}
                  </span>
                  <span class="text-body-secondary ms-2">
                    &mdash; after Activity {c.after}
                  </span>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Bubble Sort is the more interesting of the two.</b> It beats
            selection sort on one kind of input and loses to it on another, and
            the challenge is to say which one you would actually ship.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="start-here">
          <h2>If the Part 5 Quiz Went Badly</h2>

          <p>
            These three are the repair kit. <b>They count toward your five</b>
            &mdash; this is not extra work, it is a different order to do it in.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>
              <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-2/cc-roll-call">Roll Call</a>
              &mdash; linear search, and why it is sometimes the only option
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-2/cc-seat-finder">Seat Finder</a>
              &mdash; binary search, and the sorted requirement
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-2/cc-step-counter">Step Counter</a>
              &mdash; efficiency, once the first two are solid
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>For <b>each</b> challenge you complete, submit:</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              <b>Your requirements list</b> &mdash; including which algorithm you
              chose and what in the brief told you it was allowed.
            </li>
            <li><b>Your program file.</b></li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            This set is worth <b>5 points</b>, graded on completion. Extra
            credit is on top and does not replace any of the five.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1 class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash">
          Commence Challenges
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
